  "output": "## Codebase reality check\n\nBefore the category-by-category breakdown: this repo is at **Phase 1 / early Phase 4** of the AGENTS.md build order. `engine/pipeline/*.py`, `engine/agent/graph.py`, `engine/agent/prompts.py`, `engine/db.py`, `engine/main.py`, and `engine/eval/harness.py` are all `raise NotImplementedError(...)` stubs. There is no implemented matching, detection, resolution, or DB-write code yet, and the web console only has `sources` (list) and two marketing pages built — no `runs`, `conflicts`, `rules`, or `audit` routes exist.\n\nSo: there are no real N+1 queries, memory leaks, or redundant computations to fix in the engine today — that code doesn't run yet. Below I split findings into **(A) real issues in code that exists** and **(B) concrete guardrails for the stub functions that are next on the build order**, since those are exactly where these five failure modes will appear if not designed in now. I'm citing the actual stub signatures so this is actionable when you implement them, not speculative.\n\n---\n\n## A. Real findings in existing code\n\n### A1. Latent double-query in `lib/server/sources.ts`\n`web/lib/server/sources.ts:23-30` — `countSourcesBySide()` calls `listSources()` internally, which re-runs the full `select().from(sources)` query. No page currently calls both functions in the same request (`sources/page.tsx` uses `listSources` and computes its own `.length`; `get-started/page.tsx` uses only `countSourcesBySide`), so there's no active double-query today — but it's a trap the moment someone adds a \"3 configured (2 CRM / 1 warehouse)\" summary to the sources page header.\n\n```ts\n// web/lib/server/sources.ts — derive counts from an already-fetched list\n// instead of re-querying\nexport function countBySide(all: Source[]) {\n  return {\n    crm: all.filter((s) => s.kind === \"salesforce\" || s.kind === \"hubspot\").length,\n    warehouse: all.filter((s) => s.kind === \"databricks\" || s.kind === \"snowflake\").length,\n    synthetic: all.filter((s) => s.kind === \"synthetic\").length,\n  };\n}\n// callers that already have `sources` pass it in; get-started/page.tsx calls\n// listSources() once and derives both the list and the counts from it.\n```\n\n### A2. No per-request memoization on Drizzle reads\nNext.js only auto-dedupes `fetch()` calls per request; a plain async function like `listSources()` does not get that for free. Right now every page calls it at most once, so it's not costing anything — but `ConsoleLayout` (`web/app/(console)/layout.tsx`) renders `NavRail`, and as you build `runs`/`conflicts`/`rules` pages that also need source or ruleset lookups (e.g., to render \"Salesforce (prod)\" next to a conflict), you'll likely end up calling `listSources()` from both a layout-level component and a page-level component in the same tree — two round trips to Postgres per request. Wrap read functions in React's `cache()` now, while there's only one call site, so it's free later:\n\n```ts\nimport { cache } from \"react\";\n\nexport const listSources = cache(async (): Promise<Source[]> => {\n  await connection();\n  const rows = await getDb().select().from(sources).orderBy(sources.createdAt);\n  return rows.map((row) => sourceSchema.parse(row));\n});\n```\n\n### A3. `getDb()` connection caching — already correct\n`web/lib/db/index.ts` caches the `postgres()` client and Drizzle instance on `globalThis`, specifically to survive Next.js HMR reloads in dev. No change needed — flagging this because it's the thing that's usually wrong (a fresh pool per HMR reload exhausting Supabase's connection limit), and here it's already handled correctly.\n\n---\n\n## B. Guardrails for the stub functions you're about to implement\n\nThese aren't bugs — they're the specific spots where AGENTS.md's own design intent (O(n·k) blocking, a 400-row keyboard-navigable queue, prompt caching as \"the single largest cost lever\") will be undermined by the natural first-draft implementation. Each maps to a named stub.\n\n### B1. N+1 risk: the conflict queue join (`conflicts/route.ts`, not yet built)\nThe diff row needs `conflict → match → entityA/entityB → source name` for every row. AGENTS.md explicitly designs the queue for \"a reviewer working a queue of 400 conflicts\" — a naive per-row fetch loop is 400 × ~4 queries. Build the queue query as one join from the start:\n\n```ts\n// web/lib/server/conflicts.ts (when you build it)\nconst rows = await getDb()\n  .select({\n    conflict: conflicts,\n    match: matches,\n    entityA: alias(entities, \"entityA\"),\n    entityB: alias(entities, \"entityB\"),\n    sourceA: alias(sources, \"sourceA\"),\n    sourceB: alias(sources, \"sourceB\"),\n  })\n  .from(conflicts)\n  .innerJoin(matches, eq(conflicts.matchId, matches.id))\n  .innerJoin(alias(entities, \"entityA\"), eq(matches.entityAId, entities.id))\n  .innerJoin(alias(entities, \"entityB\"), eq(matches.entityBId, entities.id))\n  .innerJoin(alias(sources, \"sourceA\"), ...)\n  .innerJoin(alias(sources, \"sourceB\"), ...)\n  .where(eq(conflicts.runId, runId));\n```\n\nOne query, not O(n).\n\n### B2. Redundant computation: `blocking.py` embedding calls\n`engine/pipeline/blocking.py:block/score` are stubs, but the docstring already states the constraint (\"O(n·k), NEVER compare all pairs\"). The second-order trap once implemented: calling `sentence-transformers` `.encode()` once per entity in a loop instead of batching. Model-forward overhead dominates at small batch sizes — batch-encode both sides once, build the FAISS index once per run, and only then do top-k lookups per unmatched entity:\n\n```python\ndef block(side_a, side_b, *, top_k=TOP_K):\n    unmatched_a, unmatched_b = _exact_key_match(side_a, side_b)  # cheap, first\n    if not unmatched_b:\n        return []\n    embeddings_b = model.encode([e.normalized_name for e in unmatched_b], batch_size=256)\n    index = faiss.IndexFlatIP(embeddings_b.shape[1])\n    index.add(embeddings_b)\n    embeddings_a = model.encode([e.normalized_name for e in unmatched_a], batch_size=256)\n    _, neighbours = index.search(embeddings_a, top_k)  # one search call, not one per entity\n    ...\n```\n\n### B3. Caching: prompt caching on the ruleset block (`agent/prompts.py`)\n`build_ruleset_block` and `build_conflict_block` are stubs, but the file already states the ruleset block must be \"byte-identical across every call in a run\" to hit Anthropic's prompt cache. When you implement `resolve()` in `agent/graph.py`, make sure the ruleset block is serialized once per run (not re-stringified per conflict — even identical *content* re-serialized with different key ordering or float formatting will miss the cache) and passed as a `cache_control: {\"type\": \"ephemeral\"}` block ahead of the per-conflict suffix:\n\n```python\ndef build_ruleset_block(rules: list[SurvivorshipRule]) -> str:\n    # build once per run, reuse the exact string for every conflict —\n    # do not rebuild per-call even if the rules list is the same object\n    ...\n\n# in resolve():\nruleset_block = build_ruleset_block(rules)  # once per batch, not per conflict\nfor conflict in conflicts:\n    messages = [\n        {\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": ruleset_block, \"cache_control\": {\"type\": \"ephemeral\"}},\n        {\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": build_conflict_block(conflict)},\n    ]\n```\n\n### B4. Memory leak risk: run-status polling (TanStack Query, not yet built)\nAGENTS.md reserves TanStack Query for exactly one thing — polling `GET /api/runs/:runId/status`. The classic leak here is a `setInterval` in a `useEffect` that outlives the component, or a poll that never stops once `status` reaches a terminal state. Use `refetchInterval` as a function so React Query owns the lifecycle and stops itself:\n\n```tsx\nconst TERMINAL: RunStatus[] = [\"complete\", \"failed\"];\n\nconst { data } = useQuery({\n  queryKey: [\"run-status\", runId],\n  queryFn: () => fetchRunStatus(runId),\n  refetchInterval: (query) =>\n    query.state.data && TERMINAL.includes(query.state.data.status) ? false : 2000,\n});\n```\n\nNo manual `useEffect`/`clearInterval` needed, and it can't keep polling a finished run.\n\n### B5. Unnecessary re-renders: the conflict queue (`ConflictDiffRow`, not yet built)\nAGENTS.md requires `j`/`k`/`a`/`e`/`o` keyboard nav over up to ~400 rows. The natural first draft — a `selectedIndex` `useState` in the parent queue component — re-renders all 400 `ConflictDiffRow`s on every keypress. Wrap rows in `React.memo` and pass only `isSelected: boolean` (not the raw index) as a prop, so a row only re-renders when its own selection state flips:\n\n```tsx\nexport const ConflictDiffRow = memo(function ConflictDiffRow({\n  conflict, isSelected, onApprove, onEscalate, onOverride,\n}: ConflictDiffRowProps) {\n  /* ... */\n});\n```\nKeep `onApprove`/etc. stable with `useCallback` keyed by `conflictId`, not recreated per render of the parent.\n\n### B6. Engine DB writes: connection reuse in `db.py`\n`write_entities`, `write_matches`, `write_conflicts` are separate stub functions but will all be called from `main.py:execute_run` for the same run. Open one `psycopg` connection (or pool) per run and pass it through, rather than each `write_*` function opening/closing its own — with thousands of `entities` rows per run, per-call connection setup is the dominant cost, not the inserts themselves. Use `copy` or executemany-style batch inserts, not row-by-row `INSERT`.\n\n---\n\n## What I'd skip for now\nClient components (`nav-rail.tsx`, `pipeline-strip.tsx`, `get-started-hero.tsx`) are small, static, and already correctly scoped with `\"use client\"` at the leaf per AGENTS.md's own rule — no re-render issue worth touching. Next.js's React Compiler (`experimental.reactCompiler` in `next.config.ts`) isn't enabled; worth considering once the conflict queue exists to reduce manual `memo`/`useCallback` bookkeeping, but that's a new-dependency decision that needs your sign-off per AGENTS.md's dependency policy — flagging it, not adding it.\n",
